WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.280
The struggle against Thanos might have gone
very differently if screenwriters Christopher

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.880
Markus and Stephen McFeely had gotten their
way.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.900
In an interview with The New York Times, the
pair reveal that they almost introduced a

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:17.300
decades-old Marvel character in Avengers:
Infinity War, one with vast cosmic powers.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.120
In fact, that might even be understating the
case.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:25.220
The character in question is the multi-dimensional
entity known as the Living Tribunal, and the

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.780
scope of its powers over the multiverse are
really quite tremendous.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:33.980
Unlike nearly every other character in Marvel
lore, the Living Tribunal is a singular entity,

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.969
with no alternate counterparts in different
universes.

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:40.600
Instead, it has dominion over the entire Multiverse.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.940
No matter how you slice it, this entity is
kind of a big deal.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:48.920
The Living Tribunal is usually depicted as
a giant, golden, humanoid figure with three

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:54.840
faces, each one representing a different aspect
of the being: Equity, Necessity, and Vengeance.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.440
The Living Tribunal seeks to maintain balance
and order throughout the entire Multiverse,

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.699
and all three of its faces must agree that
an action is necessary before intervening

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:05.489
in the affairs of mere mortals.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:10.780
However, once it does decide an intervention
is in order, its authority is absolute.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:15.240
It's all pretty heady stuff, but it's easy
to see why Markus and McFeely toyed with the

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.960
idea of throwing such a fascinating character
into the mix for Infinity War.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.740
But seeing as the film and Avengers: Endgame
are already stuffed with plenty of characters,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:27.060
it's also easy to see why Marvel Studios ultimately
nixed the idea.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.080
Intriguingly, Markus and McFeely suggested
that head honcho Kevin Feige might have another

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.230
reason for omitting the character from the
action: Namely, he wants to save it for a

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:37.320
future film.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.900
Asked if there were any characters the duo
wanted for Infinity War and Endgame that they

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.100
couldn't have, Markus told The New York Times:

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:51.520
McFeely continued:

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.299
The fight on Titan was quite eventful enough,
and the sudden appearance of an as-yet-unseen

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:08.740
golden god with three faces might have totally
thrown moviegoers for a loop.

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.680
However, Markus went on to say:

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.960
McFeely says then openly worried that they'd
just spoiled a big surprise.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.920
And that's when Markus suggested that the
Living Tribunal could have his very own streaming

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:22.900
show.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.460
McFeely even proposed the show would be similar
to Judge Judy.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.260
But really, that's just silly.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.460
"Are you trying to justify to me the fact
that you're an idiot?"

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:36.520
Perhaps Markus and McFeely did blab a bit
too freely, because we can think of a perfect

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:41.330
vehicle for the Living Tribunal to receive
a proper introduction to the MCU: The Eternals

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.550
deals with a race of immortal, superpowered
beings created millions of years ago by the

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:50.160
Celestials and tasked with defending the Earth
from their evil counterparts, the Deviants.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.420
Think about it: an appearance by the Living
Tribunal in The Eternals wouldn't feel out

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:58.260
of place at all, where it could've been a
little jarring if this entity just popped

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.700
in to Infinity War to assist the good guys.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.660
Even if they could've used the help...

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:17.099
At any rate, Markus and McFeely clearly have
so many ideas, they couldn't possibly have

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:21.180
all been covered in Infinity War and Endgame,
even though they're two of the biggest event

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.660
films of all time.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.960
Here's hoping Endgame isn't their MCU swan
song.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:30.560
After all, the six films they've written so
far, all three Captain America movies, Thor:

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.370
The Dark World, Infinity War, and Endgame,
add up to more than a quarter of the MCU's

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.460
entire output.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.800
And all those films pretty much landed right
on the money, no?

